4. Workflow — Step by Step
4.1 Prerequisites
Install Git, Docker, kubectl, Minikube, and the Argo CD CLI, then verify each installation:
git --version
docker --version
kubectl version --client
minikube version
argocd version --client
4.2 Start the Kubernetes Cluster
minikube start
kubectl get nodes
Expected result: the node should be in a Ready state.
4.3 Create Kubernetes Manifests
Deployment — manifests/deployment.yaml:
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: nginx-demo
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx-demo
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx-demo
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx:1.25
          ports:
            - containerPort: 80
Service — manifests/service.yaml:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: nginx-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: nginx-demo
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80
  type: NodePort
Commit and push the manifests to the repository:
git add manifests/
git commit -m "Add Kubernetes manifests"
git push origin main
4.4 Install Argo CD
kubectl create namespace argocd
kubectl apply -n argocd \
  -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml
kubectl get pods -n argocd
Expected result: all Argo CD pods should be in a Running state.
4.5 Access the Argo CD UI
kubectl port-forward svc/argocd-server -n argocd 8080:443
Then open https://localhost:8080 in a browser.
4.6 Connect the Git Repository
Create a new Argo CD Application with the following settings:
•	Application Name: nginx-demo
•	Repo URL: https://github.com/<username>/argocd-gitops-demo
•	Path: /manifests
•	Destination: https://kubernetes.default.svc
•	Namespace: default
4.7 Configure Automated Sync
Enable the following Argo CD sync policies:
•	Automated Sync — automatically applies changes pushed to Git
•	Self Heal — automatically corrects manual/out-of-band drift
•	Prune — removes resources no longer defined in Git
4.8 Validate the Deployment
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get pods
kubectl get services
Expected result: the Deployment, Pods, and Service should all be created successfully.
4.9 Test GitOps Sync
Change the replica count in deployment.yaml:
replicas: 3
Commit and push the change:
git commit -am "Scale nginx to 3 replicas"
git push origin main
Argo CD detects the change and syncs automatically. Verify with:
kubectl get pods
4.10 Test Drift Detection
Manually scale the deployment outside of Git:
kubectl scale deployment nginx-demo --replicas=1
Argo CD detects this as OutOfSync relative to the desired state in Git. With Self Heal enabled, it automatically restores the deployment back to the declared 3 replicas.
4.11 Git-Based Rollback
If a new image or change causes a failure, revert the commit in Git:
git revert <commit-id>
git push origin main
Argo CD automatically rolls the cluster back to the previous, known-good state.
5. Argo CD Components
Component	Responsibility
API Server	Exposes the CLI and UI, and serves as the gRPC/REST interface for all client operations
Repo Server	Maintains a local cache of the Git repository and generates Kubernetes manifests from it
Application Controller	Continuously compares live cluster state with the desired Git state and reconciles differences
Redis	Provides caching to improve performance of repeated operations
6. Key GitOps Concepts
•	Single Source of Truth — Git holds the desired state of the system
•	Declarative Configuration — infrastructure and application state described in YAML
•	Continuous Reconciliation — the live state is constantly compared to the Git state
•	Automated Sync — changes in Git are automatically applied to the cluster
•	Drift Detection & Self-Healing — manual/out-of-band changes are detected and corrected
•	Rollback & Auditability — every change is a Git commit, making history and rollback simple
7. Jenkins + Argo CD Integration
The project keeps a clean separation of concerns between Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment:
•	Jenkins (CI): builds the application, runs tests, performs security scans, and pushes the container image to Amazon ECR
•	Jenkins then updates the GitOps repository with the new image tag
•	Argo CD (CD): detects the change in the GitOps repository and syncs it to the Kubernetes cluster
This separation means developers and CI tooling never interact with the cluster directly — all deployments happen exclusively through Git, improving security, traceability, and consistency.
